{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc 'cat /input/INPUT.md'","aggregated_output":"# Audit input\n\n## Exposition\n\nLet a_n=1/(n+1) for natural n. Each term is positive and at most 1. The sequence is decreasing. The terms tend to zero, so the series sum_n a_n converges. Also for every fixed N, the sum of the first N terms is finite. Audit the assertions and the supplied formal argument.\n\n## Supplied formal proposition and argument\n\nThe formal argument proves positivity, the upper bound, decreasing order and convergence of a_n to zero. It states finiteness of each finite sum. It supplies no convergence theorem for the infinite series.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"agent_message","text":"{\n  \"verdict\": \"discrepancy\",\n  \"statementAssessment\": \"The termwise claims and finite-sum claim are true. The claim that the series converges is false: terms tending to zero is necessary but not sufficient for series convergence.\",\n  \"methodAssessment\": \"The rendered formal argument covers the termwise claims and finite sums, but supplies no argument for convergence of the infinite series. The stated implication from terms tending to zero to series convergence is invalid.\",\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"sourceAssertion\": \"“The terms tend to zero, so the series sum_n a_n converges.”\",\n      \"formalCounterpart\": \"The rendered argument proves that a_n tends to zero and supplies no convergence theorem for the infinite series.\",\n      \"classification\": \"false statement; unsupported implication\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Here a_n = 1/(n+1), so the series is the harmonic series with its first term omitted, and it diverges.\",\n      \"evidence\": \"For each integer k ≥ 0, the terms with 2^k ≤ n+1 < 2^(k+1) number 2^k and each is at least 1/2^(k+1). Each such block therefore sums to at least 1/2. The partial sums are unbounded.\",\n      \"uncertainty\": \"None.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"examinedAssertions\": [\n    {\n      \"sourceAssertion\": \"“Each term is positive” and “at most 1.”\",\n      \"assessment\": \"Faithful and true.\",\n      \"evidence\": \"For every natural n, n+1 > 0 and 0 < 1/(n+1) ≤ 1. The rendered argument says it proves positivity and the upper bound.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"sourceAssertion\": \"“The sequence is decreasing.”\",\n      \"assessment\": \"Faithful and true (indeed, strictly decreasing).\",\n      \"evidence\": \"1/(n+2) < 1/(n+1) for every natural n. The rendered argument says it proves decreasing order.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"sourceAssertion\": \"“The terms tend to zero.”\",\n      \"assessment\": \"Faithful and true.\",\n      \"evidence\": \"1/(n+1) → 0 as n → ∞. The rendered argument says it proves this limit.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"sourceAssertion\": \"“For every fixed N, the sum of the first N terms is finite.”\",\n      \"assessment\": \"Faithful and true.\",\n      \"evidence\": \"A finite sum of N rational terms is finite; the rendered argument states that it proves finiteness of each finite sum.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"sourceAssertion\": \"“The terms tend to zero, so the series … converges.”\",\n      \"assessment\": \"Not faithful to the mathematics: the conclusion is false for this sequence.\",\n      \"evidence\": \"The block lower bound in the finding above shows unbounded partial sums.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"omissions\": [\n    \"The rendered formal argument supplies no proof or theorem establishing convergence of the infinite series.\"\n  ],\n  \"toolsUsed\": [\n    \"Read /input/INPUT.md with a shell command.\"\n  ],\n  \"checkingStatus\": \"Mathematical audit of the supplied exposition and rendered argument only. No Lean checks were run or inferred.\"\n}"}}
